tary textbook of Roman law.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gaius-antonius-hybrida"><span class="enc-name">Gaius Antonius Hybrida</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gaius Antonius Hybrida was a Roman politician, uncle of Mark Antony, who was consul with Cicero in 63 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gaius-cassius-longinus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gaius-cassius-longinus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gaius Cassius Longinus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gaius Cassius Longinus was a Roman senator and general, one of the leaders of the conspiracy that assassinated Julius Caesar in 44 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gaius-claudius-pulcher-consul-177-bce"><span class="enc-name">Gaius Claudius Pulcher (consul 177 BCE)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gaius Claudius Pulcher was a Roman politician and general who served as consul in 177 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gaius-cornelius-gallus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gaius-cornelius-gallus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gaius Cornelius Gallus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gaius Cornelius Gallus was a Roman poet and soldier who became the first prefect of Egypt after its annexation in 30 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gaius-gracchus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gaius-gracchus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gaius Gracchus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gaius Gracchus was a Roman tribune of the plebs in 123 and 122 BCE who continued the reforms of his brother Tiberius.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gaius-hostilius-mancinus"><span class="enc-name">Gaius Hostilius Mancinus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gaius Hostilius Mancinus was a Roman consul of 137 BCE who commanded against Numantia in the Numantine War.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gaius-julius-lacer"><span class="enc-name">Gaius Julius Lacer</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gaius Julius Lacer was a Roman architect of the early 2nd century CE, known as the builder of the Alcántara Bridge over the Tagus in Lusitania, completed under Trajan…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gaius-julius-sohaemus"><span class="enc-name">Gaius Julius Sohaemus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gaius Julius Sohaemus was the last king of Emesa in Syria, ruling from 54 CE as a client of Rome.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gaius-laelius"><span class="enc-name">Gaius Laelius</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gaius Laelius was a Roman general and statesman, the close friend and lieutenant of Publius Cornelius Scipio Africanus in the Second Punic War.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gaius-maecenas"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gaius-maecenas.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gaius Maecenas</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gaius Maecenas was a Roman statesman and friend of Augustus, descended from an Etruscan noble family of Arretium.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gaius-marius"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gaius-marius.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gaius Marius</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gaius Marius was a Roman general and politician who held the consulship seven times.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gaius-marius-the-younger"><span class="enc-name">Gaius Marius the Younger</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gaius Marius the Younger was a Roman politician, the son, probably by adoption, of the general Gaius Marius.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gaius-papius-mutilus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gaius-papius-mutilus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gaius Papius Mutilus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gaius Papius Mutilus was a Samnite general and one of the two consuls of the rebel Italian confederation in the Social War of 91–87 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gaius-petronius-prefect-of-egypt"><span class="enc-name">Gaius Petronius (prefect of Egypt)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gaius Petronius was Roman prefect of Egypt in the 20s BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gaius-poppaeus-sabinus"><span class="enc-name">Gaius Poppaeus Sabinus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gaius Poppaeus Sabinus was a Roman senator, consul in 9 CE, who governed Moesia for about twenty-four years under Augustus and Tiberius, also holding Macedonia and…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gaius-porcius-cato-consul-114-bce"><span class="enc-name">Gaius Porcius Cato (consul 114 BCE)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gaius Porcius Cato was a Roman politician, a grandson of Cato the Elder, who was consul in 114 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gaius-scribonius-curio-tribune-50-bce"><span class="enc-name">Gaius Scribonius Curio (tribune 50 BCE)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gaius Scribonius Curio was a Roman politician who, as tribune of the plebs in 50 BCE, defended Julius Caesar's interests in the Senate in the months before the civil war.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gaius-sempronius-tuditanus"><span class="enc-name">Gaius Sempronius Tuditanus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gaius Sempronius Tuditanus was a Roman politician and writer who served as consul in 129 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gaius-sextius-calvinus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gaius-sextius-calvinus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gaius Sextius Calvinus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gaius Sextius Calvinus was a Roman consul of 124 BCE who campaigned against the Salyes of southern Gaul at the request of Massalia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gaius-suetonius-paulinus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gaius-suetonius-paulinus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gaius Suetonius Paulinus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gaius Suetonius Paulinus was a Roman general and governor of Britain from about 58 to 61 CE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gaius-valerius-caburus"><span class="enc-name">Gaius Valerius Caburus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gaius Valerius Caburus was a noble of the Gallic Helvii who received Roman citizenship from the governor Gaius Valerius Flaccus, taking his patron's names.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gaius-valerius-donnotaurus"><span class="enc-name">Gaius Valerius Donnotaurus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gaius Valerius Donnotaurus was the leading man of the Helvii, a Gallic people allied to Rome, in 52 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gaius-valerius-flaccus"><span class="enc-name">Gaius Valerius Flaccus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gaius Valerius Flaccus was a Roman politician, consul in 93 BCE, who governed provinces in Spain and Gaul for an unusually long period in the 90s and 80s BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gaius-valerius-triarius"><span class="enc-name">Gaius Valerius Triarius</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gaius Valerius Triarius was a Roman legate under Lucullus in the Third Mithridatic War.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gajah-mada"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gajah-mada.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gajah Mada</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gajah Mada was the chief minister of the Majapahit Empire in the fourteenth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gajapati-dynasty"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gajapati-dynasty.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gajapati dynasty</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">The Gajapati dynasty, or Suryavamsi Gajapatis, ruled Odisha in eastern India from 1434, when Kapilendra Deva seized power from the Eastern Gangas, until about 1541.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gajayana"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gajayana.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gajayana</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gajayana was a king of Kanjuruhan in East Java in the mid-8th century, known from the Dinoyo inscription of 760.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/gajpat-singh"><span class="enc-name">Gajpat Singh</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gajpat Singh was a Phulkian Sikh chief who seized Jind and surrounding territory around 1763 after the fall of Mughal Sirhind, founding the Jind State.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gaki-sherocho"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gaki-sherocho.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gaki Sherocho</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Gaki Sherocho was the last king of Kaffa, in south-western Ethiopia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gal-vihara"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gal-vihara.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gal Vihara</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Gal Vihara is a rock temple at Polonnaruwa in Sri Lanka, made under King Parakramabahu I in the 12th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/galapagos-islands"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/galapagos-islands.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Galápagos Islands</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Galápagos Islands are a volcanic archipelago in the Pacific Ocean about 1,000 km west of the coast of Ecuador, which annexed them in 1832.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/galata"><span class="enc-name">Galata</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Galata, known in the Middle Ages also as Pera, is a quarter of Istanbul on the north side of the Golden Horn.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/galata-tower"><span class="enc-name">Galata Tower</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Galata Tower is a medieval stone tower in the Galata quarter of Istanbul.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/galati"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/galati.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Galați</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Galați is a city and port on the lower Danube in eastern Romania, in the historical region of Moldavia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/galawdewos"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/galawdewos.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Galawdewos</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Galawdewos was emperor of Ethiopia in the mid-sixteenth century, the son of Dawit II.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/galaxidi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/galaxidi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Galaxidi</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Galaxidi is a small port town in Phocis, central Greece, on the northern shore of the Gulf of Corinth.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/galba"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/galba.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Galba</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Galba was a Roman senator who, as governor of Hispania Tarraconensis, was proclaimed emperor by his troops in 68 CE during the revolt against Nero.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/galba-suessiones"><span class="enc-name">Galba (king of the Suessiones)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Galba was king of the Suessiones, a Belgic people of northern Gaul, in the time of Julius Caesar.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/galbert-of-bruges"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/galbert-of-bruges.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Galbert of Bruges</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Galbert of Bruges was a cleric and notary in the service of the counts of Flanders.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/galdan-boshugtu-khan"><span class="enc-name">Galdan Boshugtu Khan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Galdan Boshugtu Khan was ruler of the Dzungar Khanate from 1671 to 1697.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/galdan-tseren"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/galdan-tseren.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Galdan Tseren</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Galdan Tseren was ruler of the Dzungar Khanate from 1727 to 1745, the son and successor of Tsewang Rabtan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/galeazzo-ciano"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/galeazzo-ciano.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Galeazzo Ciano</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Galeazzo Ciano was an Italian Fascist politician, Benito Mussolini's son-in-law and foreign minister from 1936 to 1943.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/galeazzo-ii-visconti"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/galeazzo-ii-visconti.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Galeazzo II Visconti</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Galeazzo II Visconti was co-ruler of the Lordship of Milan from 1354 with his brother Bernabò, governing its western half.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/galeazzo-malatesta"><span class="enc-name">Galeazzo Malatesta</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Galeazzo Malatesta, nicknamed 'the Inept', was lord of Pesaro from 1429 to 1445.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/galeazzo-maria-sforza"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/galeazzo-maria-sforza.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Galeazzo Maria Sforza</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Galeazzo Maria Sforza was Duke of Milan from 1466 to 1476, eldest son of Francesco Sforza and Bianca Maria Visconti.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/galen"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/galen.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Galen</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Galen was a Greek physician of the second century CE whose writings dominated medicine for centuries.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/galerius"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/galerius.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Galerius</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Galerius was a Roman emperor of the Tetrarchy, made Caesar under Diocletian in 293 and senior emperor in the east from 305 to 311.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/galeshewe"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/galeshewe.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Galeshewe</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Galeshewe was a chief of the Batlhaping in the northern Cape, South Africa, who resisted colonial expansion.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/galich-kostroma-oblast"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/galich-kostroma-oblast.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Galich, Kostroma Oblast</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Galich is a small town on the shore of Lake Galich in Kostroma Oblast, Russia, first mentioned in the 12th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/galicia"><span class="enc-name">Galicia</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Galicia is a historical region in east-central Europe, now divided between Poland and Ukraine.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/galician-portuguese"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/galician-portuguese.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Galician-Portuguese</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Galician-Portuguese was the Romance language of medieval Galicia and northern Portugal, from which modern Galician and Portuguese developed.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/galicians"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/galicians.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Galicians</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Galicians are the people of Galicia, in north-western Spain, whose language, Galician, is closely related to Portuguese.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/galilean-moons"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/galilean-moons.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Galilean moons</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">The Galilean moons are the four largest moons of Jupiter, Io, Europa, Ganymede and Callisto, discovered by Galileo Galilei with his telescope in January 1610.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/galilee"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/galilee.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Galilee</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Galilee is a region in the north of present-day Israel.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/galileo-galilei.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Galileo Galilei</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Galileo Galilei was an Italian astronomer, physicist and mathematician whose telescopic discoveries of 1609–1610, including the moons of Jupiter and the phases of…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/galle"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/galle.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Galle</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Galle is a port city on the south-western coast of Sri Lanka.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/galle-fort"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/galle-fort.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Galle Fort</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Galle Fort is a fortified old town on the south-west coast of Sri Lanka.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/galleon"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/galleon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Galleon</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">A galleon was a large, multi-decked sailing ship used from the sixteenth century for war and long-distance trade.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/galley"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/galley.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Galley</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">A galley is a warship propelled mainly by oars, dominant in Mediterranean warfare for centuries.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gallia-aquitania"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gallia-aquitania.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gallia Aquitania</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Gallia Aquitania was a province of the Roman Empire in south-western Gaul, organised under Augustus.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gallia-belgica"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gallia-belgica.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gallia Belgica</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Gallia Belgica was a Roman province in north-eastern Gaul, organised under Augustus from the lands of the Belgae conquered by Julius Caesar.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gallia-lugdunensis"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gallia-lugdunensis.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gallia Lugdunensis</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Gallia Lugdunensis was a Roman province of Gaul organised under Augustus, named after its capital Lugdunum (Lyon).</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gallia-narbonensis"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gallia-narbonensis.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gallia Narbonensis</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Gallia Narbonensis was the Roman province of southern Gaul between the Alps and the Pyrenees, formed after Roman campaigns against the Allobroges and Arverni around 121…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/gallic-empire"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/gallic-empire.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Gallic Empire</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">The Gallic Empire was a breakaway state in the western provinces of the Roman Empire, including Gaul, Britain and for a time Spain, from 260 to 274 CE.</span></a></li></ul>
